    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Y-Intercepts of a Hyperbola",
    "problem": "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25}=1.$",
    "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =0 \\\\ (y-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-5 & =0 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $(0,5).$",
